      "model": "claude-sonnet-5",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "They describe different things. Independent events don't change each other's chances, so $P(A \\cap B) = P(A)P(B)$.",
          "citations": [
            {
              "type": "search_result_location",
              "source": "chunk:{{Two events}}",
              "title": "{{title}}",
              "cited_text": "{{text}}",
              "search_result_index": 0,
              "start_block_index": 0,
              "end_block_index": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " Mutually exclusive events can't happen together, so $P(A \\cap B) = 0$.",
          "citations": [
            {
              "type": "search_result_location",
              "source": "chunk:{{Mutually exclusive means}}",
              "title": "{{title}}",
              "cited_text": "{{text}}",
              "search_result_index": 0,
              "start_block_index": 0,
              "end_block_index": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "\n\nWhen both events have positive probability, mutually exclusive events are never independent: knowing $A$ happened tells you $B$ didn't.",
          "citations": [
            {
              "type": "search_result_location",
              "source": "chunk:{{can't be independent}}",
              "title": "{{title}}",
              "cited_text": "{{text}}",
              "search_result_index": 0,
              "start_block_index": 0,
              "end_block_index": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "\n\nExample from your notes: on one die, even and odd are mutually exclusive and dependent. On two dice, \"first die even\" and \"second die even\" are independent.",
          "citations": null
        }
      ],
